WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Why can a strawberry runner make a copy, while brothers and sisters are different?

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.820
Watch one garden tell both stories.

3
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.880
A runner stretches from one plant and roots nearby.

4
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.240
It has one parent.

5
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:16.940
This is asexual reproduction.

6
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.760
The new plant usually carries the same genetic information.

7
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.800
Sexual reproduction takes another path.

8
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.360
Two sex cells join, usually one from each of two parents.

9
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.000
In animals, those cells are sperm and egg.

10
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.700
Before they join, a problem must be solved.

11
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.100
Body cells carry chromosomes in pairs.

12
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.180
Joining two fully paired cells would double the chromosome number.

13
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.180
For more information, please visit www .beadaholique .com

14
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:51.163
Follow the blue and gold bars.

15
00:00:52.463 --> 00:00:54.963
Each colour marks one member of a chromosome pair.

16
00:00:56.223 --> 00:00:59.963
The pair carries the same kinds of genes in the same general places.

17
00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:04.103
One member came from the mother, and one from the father.

18
00:01:05.383 --> 00:01:07.463
A body cell keeps both members together.

19
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:13.153
Which move could let two sex cells join without doubling the chromosome number?

20
00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:19.913
Watch the bars. Should every pair stay together, or should each cell carry only

21
00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.413
half? Make your prediction.

22
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.500
Meiosis solves the problem.

23
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.380
The members of each pair separate.

24
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.740
A sex cell receives one chromosome from every pair.

25
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.000
Its chromosome number is half the body-cell number.

26
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:49.460
Here is the rule. Sexual reproduction: meiosis halves chromosome number and makes

27
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.000
mixes; fertilization restores pairs.

28
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.220
Now a sperm and egg move together.

29
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.180
Fertilization joins them into one new cell.

30
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.280
Their half sets make full pairs again.

31
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.680
Each restored pair has one chromosome from each parent.

32
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:13.080
The chromosome number returns to the usual body-cell number instead of growing with

33
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.080
every generation.

34
00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:16.863
Look again at the bar count.

35
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:20.883
Halving does not mean cutting chromosomes into pieces.

36
00:02:22.283 --> 00:02:26.423
It means placing one whole chromosome from every pair into a sex cell.

37
00:02:27.643 --> 00:02:30.423
When the cells join, the partners form pairs again.

38
00:02:31.723 --> 00:02:36.443
This movement can repeat across generations without a steady rise in chromosome

39
00:02:36.443 --> 00:02:37.443
number.

40
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:40.747
The picture used animal sperm and egg cells.

41
00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:43.507
Plants take an extra step.

42
00:02:44.847 --> 00:02:47.887
Meiosis first makes spores, and sex cells form later.

43
00:02:49.207 --> 00:02:50.947
Those sex cells still carry half.

44
00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:58.187
The simple model also has an exception: rare DNA changes can make an asexual copy

45
00:02:58.187 --> 00:02:59.187
imperfect.

46
00:02:59.917 --> 00:03:01.237
Return to the garden.

47
00:03:02.517 --> 00:03:06.897
The runner grows from one parent, so it usually carries that parent's genetic

48
00:03:06.897 --> 00:03:11.357
information. Sexual offspring begin when half sets join.

49
00:03:12.797 --> 00:03:15.257
Meiosis prevents chromosome number from doubling.

50
00:03:16.697 --> 00:03:18.357
Fertilization restores the pairs.

51
00:03:19.297 --> 00:03:22.757
The next question is why those half sets are not all alike.
